-- title:
--   Existence of a majority-favored cone in a finite block, attained at a point
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$, assigning to each point $x$ a double cone $\Gamma(x)$. For a set $Q \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ and a double cone $V$, write
--
--   $$\Gamma^{-1}_Q(V) = \{\, x \in Q : \Gamma(x) \text{ has the same underlying set as } V \,\}$$
--
--   for the fibre of $\Gamma$ over $V$ inside $Q$, and say that $V$ is *favored by majority in $Q$* when this fibre is of maximal size, i.e. $\#\Gamma^{-1}_Q(W) \le \#\Gamma^{-1}_Q(V)$ for every double cone $W$.
--
--   If $Q$ is **finite**, then such a $V$ exists; moreover it may be taken to occur in $Q$ whenever $Q$ is nonempty:
--
--   $$\exists\, V \ \bigl(\, V \text{ favored by majority in } Q \ \wedge\ (Q \ne \emptyset \Rightarrow \exists\, p \in Q,\ V = \Gamma(p)) \,\bigr).$$
--
--   The second clause is what makes the notion usable: a majority cone is not merely abstractly optimal but is realized as the cone of an actual point of the block, so the majority set is a genuine subset of $Q$ on which $\Gamma$ is constant. For empty $Q$ the statement is vacuous in the second clause and any cone is favored, all fibres being empty.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Cones are compared through their underlying sets (`carrier`), not as structures, so two double cones with different axis/angle data but the same underlying set count as equal here. Fibre sizes are `Set.encard`, valued in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$; finiteness of $Q$ is what makes the maximum exist.
